WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:06.940
Hi everyone, my name is Hillary. I'm going to talk about healthcare research in our UX study.

2
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:14.940
I focused in on this concept of government mail. Has anyone received it?

3
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:23.940
Probably at some point in your life you've had some sort of sample letter that was really confusing and it didn't exactly provide you with the next steps.

4
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:31.460
That's a lot of what my study is really based off of.

5
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:40.460
Initially, I was really focused on insurance pre-approvals and how denials took place.

6
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:46.460
I started going through that rabbit hole, doing my research, defining some MVPs.

7
00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:55.460
I eventually found myself to finding a sample letter that is affecting a major change within Medicaid.

8
00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.460
I started to focus in on Medicaid a little bit more.

9
00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.460
Holistically, I started with six interviews. I had clinical and insurer staff.

10
00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:09.460
I used six different publicly available sources. I did not conduct any patient interviews,

11
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:20.460
although a lot of the inferences and insights that I got from my clinical staff and insurer staff kind of led me to believe certain things were common amongst patients.

12
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.460
I have some of the deliverables that I need below as well.

13
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.460
We'll go through some of those in a little bit.

14
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:36.460
As you can see, I have my affinity clusters here. I have six different themes that started to emerge.

15
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:41.460
The first theme that started to emerge is who owns it.

16
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:52.460
A lot of this research, the synthesis, I was finding that there was a lot of vague handoffs and patients were unclear about next steps.

17
00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.460
They got information from insurance, but then all of a sudden the providers were having new information,

18
00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:02.460
and the providers were also then going back to insurance and validating that information.

19
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:14.460
Within this triangle of insurance physician and patient, it was pretty confusing for all parties involved, I have to say.

20
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:23.460
Insurance didn't have to be super clear with how that in-between was.

21
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:27.460
I'll talk a little bit more about it later, but there was a lot of confusion.

22
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:35.460
I wanted to really look in in this process and make things a little bit more clear for patients as well as providers.

23
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:41.460
The overall problem was that coverage can be lost.

24
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.460
It was actually already seen in Arkansas in 2018.

25
00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:49.460
18,000 people lost coverage under a work requirement.

26
00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:59.460
When I was conducting my interviews, I started to see that there was an increase in coverage lost earlier this year

27
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:07.460
and alluding to patients being lost in coverage within these new changes that were happening within the next two years.

28
00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:15.460
As you can see on the left-hand side, our projected numbers are 475,000 people that could lose coverage

29
00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:19.460
if they aren't aware of these new requirements for Medicaid.

30
00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:27.460
When I was looking at this sample letter, I noticed that it was pretty vague.

31
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:34.460
It's the first step within this Medicaid process of informing the patients, but even with that, it was pretty vague.

32
00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:40.460
I wanted to be a part of a solution that was affecting this wide net of people.

33
00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.460
The competition, what already exists?

34
00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:49.460
New York State of Health, they do have really good guidelines.

35
00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:54.460
It's just really scattered and it can be really confusing for some patients.

36
00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.460
I started looking at their page.

37
00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:02.460
I started looking at New York Health Department as well as Oklahoma Health Care Authority

38
00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:07.460
